} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{6}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{18} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{z}^{2}}{18} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {z}^{2} & =9 \\\\ z & =\\pm 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get the solutions $z=-3$ and $z=3.$\n\nTherefore, the intercepts with the $z$-axis are $(0,0,-3)$ and $(0,0,3).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378914 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186378915 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378914 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186378916 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378914 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186378917 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378914 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186378918 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378914 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186378919 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378914 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378914 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378914 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186378916 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378914 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "a327b759-622d-5e88-8ad4-130e3ada74ef" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378915 #edb/ref 1080 "$(0,0,-3)$ and $(0,0,2)$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378915 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378915 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "fac58459-fc8a-5be9-a174-357811457c1f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378916 #edb/ref 1080 "$(0,0,-3)$ and $(0,0,3)$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378916 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378916 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "472da650-36fa-5f79-a933-0ba9360f241a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378917 #edb/ref 1080 "$(0,0,-3)$ and $(0,0,1)$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378917 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378917 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "f82ff234-f117-517b-813c-c4767f7d1219" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378918 #edb/ref 1080 "$(0,0,-2)$ and $(0,0,3)$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378918 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378918 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1b6cafc0-e998-5e08-8682-7a681d233ee4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378919 #edb/ref 1080 "$(0,0,-1)$ and $(0,0,3)$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378919 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378919 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "57202985-5772-5066-a73b-46c3a4f15432" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378927 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186378928 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378927 #edb/ref 1069 "q-164308" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378927 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378927 #edb/ref 1071 "Given the sphere $(x-2)^{2}+{y}^{2}+(z+1)^{2}=4,$ what is the domain of $z=z(x,y)?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378927 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e5fe8757-d817-5a49-b319-898d54ef3e7a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378927 #edb/ref 1074 "From the equation of the sphere, we make $z$ the subject as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2}+{y}^{2}+(z+1)^{2} & =4 \\\\ (z+1)^{2} & =4-((x-2)^{2}+{y}^{2}) \\\\ z+1 & =\\pm \\sqrt{4-((x-2)^{2}+{y}^{2})} \\\\ z & =-1\\pm \\sqrt{4-((x-2)^{2}+{y}^{2})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the square root is defined only for non-negative arguments, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4-((x-2)^{2}+{y}^{2})\\ge 0 \\\\ (x-2)^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis inequality describes the interior and boundary of the circle \n\n$$\n(x-2)^{2}+{y}^{2}=4\n$$\n\nwhich is a circle of radius $2$ centered at $(2,0)$ in the $xy$-plane." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378928 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186378929 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378928 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186378930 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378928 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186378931 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378928 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186378932 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378928 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186378933 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378928 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378928 #edb/ref 107