f 1078 #edb/ref 17592186564961 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419595 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "6e468b59-ed57-5245-8552-ab699c4cd823" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419596 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186419595 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419596 #edb/ref 1069 "q-164316" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419596 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419596 #edb/ref 1071 "Given the ellipsoid $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{4}+(z-1)^{2}=1$, find the domain of $z=z(x,y).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419596 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "204288b8-790b-5fba-b88f-1aae9820b71f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419596 #edb/ref 1074 "From the ellipsoid equation, we make $z$ the subject as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{4}+(z-1)^{2} & =1 \\\\ (z-1)^{2} & =1-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}-\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{4} \\\\ z-1 & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}-\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the square root is defined only for non-negative arguments, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}-\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{4}\\ge 0 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{4}\\le 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the interior of the ellipse $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{4}=1$ and its boundary. \n\nTherefore, the domain is \n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{4}\\le 1\\}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419597 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186419598 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419597 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3276d5ad-c70a-52b0-8d3a-754d248220b5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419597 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186419596 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419598 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "38ef1b20-2bbb-5c96-ab14-7866ebfef469" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419598 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186284477 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419599 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186419601 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419599 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4bbb34e5-8d32-5bf4-847f-6d8da9